WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:07.280
all right so here is a research paper published&nbsp;
by NASA and it credits T. H. Skopinski and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:13.280
Katherine Johnson it was written at the Langley&nbsp;
research center and was written in September&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:20.800
1960. That's a year before Alan Shepard would&nbsp;
become the first American in space and before John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:26.720
Glenn would become the first American to orbit the&nbsp;
earth. The legacy of people like Katherine Johnson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:32.000
has helped to bring a lot of attention to the&nbsp;
work done at NASA around this time, calculating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:38.320
how to put people up in space and how to bring&nbsp;
them back down safely. The movie Hidden Figures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:44.320
especially highlighted the role of human computers&nbsp;
in that work but until now I never really knew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.840
what that work would have looked like. I knew that&nbsp;
Katherine Johnson's work as shown in the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:55.760
had something to do with orbital mechanics or&nbsp;
analytic geometry but I was curious to know what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.280
the actual calculations and equations were so&nbsp;
that's why I went looking through the archives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:06.640
to find the actual documents. This is the first&nbsp;
report that officially includes Katherine's name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:12.480
on the cover page but it's not the first report&nbsp;
she worked on it's called the determination of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:19.360
azimuth angle at burnout for placing a satellite&nbsp;
over a selected earth position. Let's go through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.680
some of the terms. The azimuth angle is measured&nbsp;
from north and is the angle that you're pointing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:32.000
when you launch a rocket, often and is limited by&nbsp;
safety we don't really want to launch rockets over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.760
populated areas or over houses and so if you're&nbsp;
launching you often want to have your azimuth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:44.560
angle such that you're going to launch out over&nbsp;
the ocean and burnout refers to the shut off of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:51.440
the rocket engine so after the burnout point the&nbsp;
orbital parameters are essentially locked in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:57.920
we can then just work with newton's laws and other&nbsp;
equations to find out where it's going to be.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:04.080
Because the azimuth angle at launch will affect&nbsp;
where the satellite is going to end up after a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:11.760
certain number of orbits, this paper is trying to&nbsp;
find out what launch azimuth angle you would need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:18.800
in order to end up at a selected earth position&nbsp;
after a set number of orbits so say you want your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:24.080
astronaut to splash down in a certain region&nbsp;
on earth so that you can go and retrieve them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:29.440
well the math in this paper is working out&nbsp;
what you would need to do at launch and able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:35.200
ensure that. They start by listing out a bunch of&nbsp;
symbols, we have things like the semi-major axis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:41.920
and the eccentricity relating to the shape of&nbsp;
the elliptical orbit and we have the inclination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:47.760
angle of the orbital plane and some of the&nbsp;
parameters make reference to the perigee location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:53.600
which is the point when the satellite is closest&nbsp;
to the center of the earth. It says here that if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:03:01.200
we know the initial conditions of speed, radial&nbsp;
distance and elevation angle at burnout those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:07.040
will all determine the orbital characteristics&nbsp;
of a satellite. So going on to work out a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:13.280
relate all of these parameters they start with a&nbsp;
bit of elementary orbital mechanics for which they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:20.400
reference An Introduction to Celestial Mechanics&nbsp;
published in 1914. These are all derivations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:27.120
working towards that ultimate relationship between&nbsp;
the azimuth angle and the longitude and latitude&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:33.920
that we wish to land at. These calculations were&nbsp;
done by hand likely by Katherine Johnson herself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:40.240
but then it says here that the results for both&nbsp;
cases are then compared with the results obtained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:48.800
by a method of a three degree of freedom&nbsp;
calculation performed on an IBM 704 electronic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:55.840
data processing machine. This would be around the&nbsp;
time that NASA got its very first computers, its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:03.360
IBM machines which would have been huge and taken&nbsp;
up an entire room and very difficult to use. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:11.040
go on to present two example orbit calculations,&nbsp;
one for a satellite that is launched westward and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:16.320
one for satellite launched eastward just to show&nbsp;
that this method is not limited by the direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:22.320
of launch. This page here is a summary of all of&nbsp;
the equations that were derived and that will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:28.240
useful in the calculation and it actually involves&nbsp;
a bit of an iterative procedure because we don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:34.320
initially know anything about the orbital angle&nbsp;
or the time it takes to get to a certain position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:40.080
we just have this first approximation for the&nbsp;
time it's taken to get to a selected point we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:47.440
feed that approximation into another equation and&nbsp;
that value into another until we start to see some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:53.040
convergence in the values we're getting for the&nbsp;
angle after we're happy with this much we can then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:59.280
finally feed the parameters into an equation to&nbsp;
find the azimuth angle and the inclination angle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:05.120
telling us what we need to launch the satellite.&nbsp;
There's also a mention of oblateness effects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:11.680
because the earth is not perfectly spherical and&nbsp;
the oblateness is taken into account with small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:18.960
modifications to the latitude and longitude values&nbsp;
after the first iteration. And these are the exact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:25.520
equations here that Katherine Johnson would&nbsp;
have been calculating in her role as a computer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:30.560
but for this paper she also had a lot to&nbsp;
do with deriving them in the first place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:37.200
In 1960 no woman at NASA had ever had her name&nbsp;
included on a report. Katherine Johnson worked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:43.600
on this report with Ted Skopinski and their&nbsp;
supervisor encouraged Skopinski to finish the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:50.480
report and just put his name on it before going&nbsp;
